# Rust
/target
**/target
# Cargo.lock IS committed: 屠つ is an application, so a reproducible dependency
# graph is wanted (Rust's guidance is to commit it for binaries, ignore for libs).

# Node / web frontend
node_modules
dist
.vite
ui/test-results/
ui/playwright-report/

# Tauri
src-tauri/target
src-tauri/gen
# Generated from the installed Visual Studio redistributable immediately before a
# Windows bundle build. The licensed DLLs belong in the installer, not source control.
src-tauri/windows-runtime

# Bundled models & sidecars (fetched, never committed)
sidecars/models
*.onnx
*.bin
*.safetensors

# User data captured during dev
*.log
/scratch

# Installer QA output: screenshots, transcripts and install logs from a sandbox run.
# Artefacts of a run rather than source, stale the moment the next build happens, and they
# carry the running machine's own paths. The harness that produces them *is* committed —
# see .installer-qa/*.ps1. Same call as ui/test-results/ above.
.installer-qa/results/
# The sandbox config those scripts generate, which hardcodes host paths.
*.wsb

# Secrets: environment files and private keys. Nothing in the project uses either today; this
# is so the first one ever created cannot be committed by accident. `.env.example`-style
# templates, if one is ever wanted, are let back in by name.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem

# Python bytecode from scripts/
__pycache__/

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json

